Since \\(xf′(x) > f(x)\\), we can rewrite this as \\( \\dfrac {f(x)}{x}=\[xf′(x)-f(x)\] \\dfrac {1}{x^{2}}\\). Let \\(F(x)= \\dfrac {f(x)}{x}\\). In its domain, \\(F(x)\\) is an increasing function. Given that \\(F(2)= \\dfrac {f(2)}{2}=0\\), the solution set of \\(f(x) < 0\\) is equivalent to the solution set of \\( \\dfrac {f(x)}{x} < 0\\), which is the same as the solution set of \\(F(x) < F(2)\\). Solving for this, we obtain the set \\(\\{x|0 < x < 2\\}\\).

